<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ba Maw</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ba Maw was a Burmese lawyer and politician who served as the first premier of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1937 to 1939. During the Japanese occupation he became head of the Japanese-sponsored State of Burma, declared independent in 1943. After the war he was briefly imprisoned and never regained real political influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ba-maw.jpg" alt="Ba Maw" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ba_Maw_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ba Maw was born on 8 February 1893 in Maubin, into a family of mixed Mon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Bamar</a> descent. His father, Shwe Kye, a Mon from Amherst, had accompanied Kinwun Mingyi U Kaung on Burmese missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 1870s. Raised a Christian, Ba Maw later became a Buddhist. He studied at Rangoon College, took an MA at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1917 and became the first English lecturer at Rangoon University. He then studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, was called to the bar at Gray's Inn in 1923 and took a doctorate at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> with a thesis in French on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ba Maw came to prominence in 1931 as lead counsel for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saya-san">Saya San</a>, whose tax revolt of December 1930 had grown into a rebellion against British rule; San was convicted and hanged. Ba Maw became education minister in 1934 and premier under the new constitution in 1937. In July 1940 he left the legislature and called on Burmese to refuse any part in the war while freedom was denied them. Arrested on 6 August 1940 under the Defence of Burma Rules, he was sentenced to a year in prison and held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogok">Mogok</a>.</p><p>He escaped from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mogok">Mogok</a> on 13 April 1942, made contact with the Japanese and on 1 August 1942 was inaugurated as head of the Burmese government. When the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> was proclaimed on 1 August 1943 he became its head of state and prime minister; it declared war on the United Kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In 1944 a few Japanese officers plotted to replace him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-nu">U Nu</a>, who refused and warned him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As British forces advanced Ba Maw fled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan">American occupation</a> authorities held him in Sugamo Prison until 1946. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> he declined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a>'s invitation to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-peoples-freedom-league">AFPFL</a> and rebuilt the Mahabama Party in opposition. Under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ne-win">Ne Win</a> he was imprisoned without charge or trial from about 1965 or 1966 to February 1968. His memoir Breakthrough in Burma: Memoirs of a Revolution, 1939–1946 was published by Yale University Press in 1968. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> in May 1977.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ba Maw” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 August 1943 Japan granted nominal independence, and <b>Ba Maw</b>, a pre-war premier, became head of state, or Adipadi, of the State of Burma, which declared war on Britain and the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-burma">State of Burma</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ma-ubin, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.73,95.65&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Ma-ubin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7153 1542 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ba Maw"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7603 1812h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7603 1812h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.73,95.65&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
